    "marks": 8,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Phytoplankton → water fleas → small fish → water birds\nPhytoplankton: producer (first trophic level)\nWater fleas: primary consumer (second trophic level)\nSmall fish: secondary consumer (third trophic level)\nWater birds: tertiary consumer (fourth trophic level)\n\n(b) Energy passes from one trophic level to the next very inefficiently, typically at about ten per cent. The rest is lost as heat from respiration, or remains in parts not eaten, in faeces and in indigestible material.\nAfter three or four transfers too little energy remains to support a viable population, so food chains rarely exceed four or five trophic levels.\n\n(c) The likely chain of consequences:\n1. Phosphate is a limiting nutrient, so a large input lets algae and phytoplankton multiply rapidly, producing an algal bloom.\n2. The surface becomes covered in algae, light cannot penetrate, and rooted water plants below die for lack of light.\n3. As the algae and plants die in quantity, decomposer bacteria multiply and break them down, consuming dissolved oxygen.\n4. Dissolved oxygen falls sharply and fish and other aerobic organisms die of oxygen shortage.\n5. Biodiversity falls and the structure of the pond ecosystem is damaged.\n\n(d) The reasoning takes only the first step of the causal chain and ignores what follows. The plants (algae) do multiply with the extra nutrient, but algae are short-lived, and when they die bacteria decompose them, consuming large amounts of dissolved oxygen. The result is not more fish but fish dying of oxygen shortage. The reasoning also ignores the bloom shading out the rooted plants below.",
