    "explanation": "(c) 是本題的判別點，考的是條件機率。四種等可能結果為 RR、Rr、rR、rr；題目已告知該子女能捲舌，即已排除 rr，餘下三種等可能，其中兩種為雜合子，故機率為三分之二。答二分之一者，是忽略了題目所給的額外資訊 —— 「已知能捲舌」把樣本空間由四項收窄至三項。此類「已知…則…」的問法在歷屆反覆出現，而失分幾乎全部源於未把已知條件用上。\n(a) 的推斷方向須寫明：兒子為 rr，其兩個 r 各來自父母一方，故父母各自必帶一個 r；而父母能捲舌，故各自必有一個 R。",
    "referenceAnswer": "(a) 父母雙方的基因型均為 Rr（雜合子）。\n推斷依據：兒子不能捲舌，屬隱性性狀，基因型必為 rr。其兩個 r 等位基因分別來自父親與母親，故父母各自必帶有一個 r。又父母二人皆能捲舌，屬顯性性狀，故各自必帶有至少一個 R。兩項合併，父母的基因型只能是 Rr。\n\n(b) Rr × Rr 的子代基因型比例為 RR : Rr : rr = 1 : 2 : 1。\n能捲舌者為 RR 及 Rr，佔四分之三。\n故機率為 3/4（0.75）。\n\n(c) 四種等可能組合為 RR、Rr、rR、rr。題目已知該子女能捲舌，故 rr 被排除，餘下 RR、Rr、rR 三種等可能情況。\n其中雜合子（Rr 或 rR）佔兩種。\n故機率為 2/3。\n須注意此處不可答 1/2 —— 題目提供了「能捲舌」這項額外資訊，必須用以收窄樣本空間。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Both parents are Rr (heterozygous).\nDeduction: the son cannot roll, a recessive phenotype, so his genotype must be rr. His two r alleles come one from each parent, so each parent must carry an r. Both parents can roll, a dominant phenotype, so each must also carry at least one R. Together these force the genotype Rr for each parent.\n\n(b) A cross Rr × Rr gives offspring in the ratio RR : Rr : rr = 1 : 2 : 1.\nThose that can roll are RR and Rr, three out of four.\nThe probability is therefore 3/4 (0.75).\n\n(c) The four equally likely combinations are RR, Rr, rR and rr. The question states that the child can roll, so rr is excluded, leaving RR, Rr and rR as three equally likely cases.\nTwo of these are heterozygous (Rr or rR).\nThe probability is therefore 2/3.\nNote that 1/2 is not acceptable here — the question supplies the extra information \"can roll\", which must be used to narrow the sample space.",
    "contentEn": "Tongue-rolling in humans is controlled by one pair of alleles: rolling (R) is dominant and non-rolling (r) is recessive.\nA couple who can both roll their tongues have a son who cannot.\n(a) State the genotypes of both parents and explain how they are deduced.\n(b) If the couple have another child, what is the probability that the child can roll its tongue?\n(c) Given that the second child can roll its tongue, what is the probability that it is heterozygous?",
    "explanationEn": "Part (c) is the discriminator and tests conditional probability. The four equally likely outcomes are RR, Rr, rR and rr; the question states that the child can roll, which removes rr and leaves three equally likely outcomes, two of them heterozygous, giving two-thirds. Answering one-half ignores the information given — \"given that it can roll\" narrows the sample space from four to three. This \"given that…\" form recurs in past papers, and almost all lost marks come from not using the stated condition.\nIn (a) the direction of the deduction must be written out: the son is rr, and his two r alleles come one from each parent, so each parent must carry an r; since both can roll, each must also carry an R."
